WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.830
I think all of the chemistry and all of the
science

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:05.620
is in service of trying to create aesthetic
beauty in a glass.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.720
When you sip from a glass of well-aged smokey
whiskey,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.480
you can almost taste the years its been sitting
in a barrel.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.380
And that’s what allows distillers to charge
in the thousands of dollars for a top-shelf bottle.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.560
So as the booze sits in a barrel for decades
and decades and decades,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.720
the wood sort of slowly dissolves into it

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.900
and, as the polymer structures that make the
wood, wood, slowly fall apart,

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.500
they shed all of these precursor chemicals
that turn into different stuff

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.480
that tastes really good.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.020
You guys want to hop aboard?

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.000
Careful getting on, it is on water.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.560
We took this boat ride deep into this jungle
to talk with Bryan Davis,

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.140
one of the co-founders of Lost Spirits Distillery.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.380
He’s part of a community of craft distillers
who have opened up shop in the last ten years,

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.920
riding a wave of interest in premium beverages.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.420
So that's actually a little top secret-ish
in terms of exactly what it’s doing.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.400
But it's part of a set of experiments that
are designed around

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.920
scaling up the technology we’ve been working
on.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.720
You’ll find a lot of oddities inside of
Davis’ distillery.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.400
Everything from a hypotonic floating carousel
to god like statues

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.560
to a dragon skull sitting in a room straight
out of the Island of Doctor Moreau.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.380
But you won’t find rows of oak barrels aging
booze.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.340
That’s because he’s developed an untraditional
way

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.320
of making spirits taste like they’ve been
sitting around for decades,

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.300
when in fact they’re a mere six days old.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.560
I got to be the first guy to recreate, effectively,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.760
the chemical signature of 15-20 year old bottles
of booze in the laboratory.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.140
Davis, who has won multiple awards for his
product,

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.560
invented a reactor that mimics the natural
aging process when booze is left in a barrel.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.320
That's sort of the thing that put us on the
map, and created the lab and the business model.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.300
This time and money-saving technology could transform
the market for aged spirits

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.200
by bringing these products within reach of
a larger community of liquor consumers.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:57.240
Davis took an unconventional path to becoming
a liquor distiller.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.100
He started making his own booze in high school
when he couldn’t get a good enough fake ID.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.320
Built sort of rudimentary still out of crap
from the garage

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.260
and made some pretty awful booze.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.740
He eventually went to art school for college
where he studied sculpture

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.240
and then went on to design for amusement parks.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.560
That jungle you saw earlier sits in a warehouse
in downtown Los Angeles

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.960
and it’s one of the many worlds that Davis
says he’s trying to recreate for drinkers

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.180
through their taste buds.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.340
I certainly still think like an artist

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.720
and it definitely influences the approach
that I take to what I'm trying to make.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.400
I think all of the chemistry and all of the
science

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.220
is in service of trying to create aesthetic
beauty in a glass.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.880
The liquor world has been forbidding to small
distillers since the repeal of prohibition.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.620
To get started in the industry, Davis had
to get creative.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.340
When he arrived in California with his significant
other Joanne Haruta,

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:47.800
who's also his business partner,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.700
they tried to get into the whiskey and rum
business,

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:54.120
but Davis says he needed almost half a million
dollars in backing to produce a three-year-old bottle.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.420
We were utterly broke.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.980
So, we started down the path of figuring out how to go get old boilers

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.900
out of apartment buildings in San Francisco on Craigslist for $500.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.880
and we built all the stills out of sheet copper
with a hammer and a torch.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.920
But they still needed warehouses and barrels.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.180
We would just be literally sitting there going,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.420
"Okay, we'll sell our first bottle of booze
when I'm 60,"

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.020
And I thought, "Well, I mean, it's only chemistry,
right?

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:17.940
I don't really know anything about chemistry,
but how hard can it be?"

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.300
So Davis went on the internet

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.060
and started watching YouTube videos of chemistry
classes taught at MIT.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.900
And every time I hit a vocabulary word I didn't
understand, I'd just pause it

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.950
then go to Wikipedia and put in the vocabulary
word and then read that.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.800
Then it would typically reference other vocabulary
words that I didn't understand.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.099
You can just sort of tunnel your way down
the rabbit hole

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.780
until all of a sudden one day you read a paper
and go,

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.340
wow, I understood everything that said, you know?

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.900
By 2013, Davis had figured out how to force
the chemical reaction called esterification,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.260
which is the main driving force behind barrel
aging.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.700
You're not like cooking with wood, it's not
like you're flavoring spirits with the barrel,

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.640
you're actually triggering a series of downstream
chemical reactions

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.400
that change the nature of the product.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.200
But he couldn’t come up with a natural way
to mimic the effects of time

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.520
and break the polymer structures in wooden
barrel

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.300
that give a well-aged bottle of booze its taste.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.659
One day, while thinking about how he had to
replace the sun damaged wooden deck

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.500
attached to his mobile home, he had an epiphany.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.920
So I'm sitting there standing on this old
worn out deck

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:20.800
and the little voice in the back of my head
is saying

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.320
you need to put a new coat of paint on the deck.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.580
But it sort of said it in science nerd terms,
right, which was, you know,

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.920
"Gee, the sunlight's doing one hell of a number
on the polymers

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.450
that are holding the structural integrity
of the wood together on your deck.

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.500
You should really put a new coat of paint
on it."

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.380
and I sort of got maybe 100 feet away and
then went like, “Huh.”

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:41.460
His idea was that by blasting wood with light

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.080
he could speed up the effects of degrading
a barrel’s wood into the alcohol.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.040
So he went to Home Depot and bought every
lighting fixture and bulb he could get his hands on.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.500
And I called my electrician, had him come
over

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.620
and we unplugged all the power from everything
on the property

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.800
and routed all the electricity into the barn.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.620
And then set up all these different light
fixtures aimed at different jars of booze

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:01.580
with the wood in them.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:05.400
And then turned them all on ran whole different
giant battery of tests.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:13.760
He also found that type of wood used in his
process impacts the taste.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.480
One of his current projects is to recreate
rum from the 1860s and 70s

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.700
aged in American chestnut barrels.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.260
The problem is that the American chestnut
tree went extinct in 1915.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:26.480
And so we went to auction and started buying
furniture made out of American chestnut

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.740
and bringing it back and slapping it on a
table saw,

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:32.460
buzzing off pieces of it so that you could
extract the usable wood

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:36.340
and toasting and charring it and using it
to try and create the profiles

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:37.920
that you would have got from barrels.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.740
Today, you can find Davis’ liquor in specialty
liquor stores

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.580
and he regularly appears at distilling conferences.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.680
The technology is talked about endlessly on
liquor blogs too.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.620
Liquor companies have lobbied for laws that
outline strict definitions

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.000
of what you may call certain types of alcohol,

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.360
so he’s had to come up with some non-traditional names.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:01.880
For example, we make, what, for all intents and
purposes,

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.040
is a very highly awarded Scotch whiskey

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.800
that we call Abomination and we label it as
a heavily peated malt

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.820
and I have no idea what that means.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.520
But Davis doesn’t mind the variety.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.560
He dreams of a future in which liquor store
shelves are lined with bottles

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:18.100
that customers don’t recognize.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:21.140
As a consumer, none of it would make any sense
and you'd have to look it up

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:22.449
and actually understand what you're buying.

00:06:22.449 --> 00:06:24.520
If you look at this from an art world's point
of view,

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.199
once upon a time sculptures were made of stone
or bronze, paintings were done in oil,

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.340
there were two styles of painting that you
could do.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:36.020
In the case of distilled spirits, we sort
of gradually evolved into that same position

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:39.720
but the industry had the advantage of being
able to consolidate after prohibition.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.600
So instead of having 10,000 small distilleries
across the United States

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:43.920
you suddenly had ten.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.860
And so all that consolidation meant these
guys made basically the same thing

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:52.320
and then, and then ultimately, they wanted
to try to legally enshrine their own monopolies

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:56.680
and so they created laws that said, "Whiskey
must be exactly this."

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:00.720
I sort of view myself as that same antagonist
to those canonistic systems

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:05.060
and have a ball trying to see how many monkey
wrenches I can throw into arguments in the systems

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.420
without breaking the laws.

